#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/cf/cf50ed91023704d55ddb9874e9c2bd081bce5ab28fb556019d038e55f32c0905.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where \n\n![](images/e3/e3892b772fb778d209832e6f5e8337186a4343ff8f550c6f8457edb9ae0311e3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/93/932bb863f1e3c1e59d7d0afc649768f193f8349c94c6e70ae1262ed5011ac34b.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133663 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429" :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](images/3e/3e5e0da0f8e23d412833c26dc9967e443fdccb98d347eaa0481f94294093341b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c29b82-3f5f-5c8e-a125-2cbdd6417d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420936} {:answer/id #uuid "47817a63-5bbe-52d7-9d9c-c60e2ec34597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420937} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0ce33d-1a7a-5210-8d79-aac72efd95ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420938} {:answer/id #uuid "b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420939} {:answer/id #uuid "be2d6e42-5df1-5c0e-95a9-7be627821491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b36fb759-7e61-5dc6-b202-80ff8e6277b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420939} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad861459-e1a0-5990-87eb-f4e42696a99f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0b5754c-fcd1-5841-875b-00aa65f001b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-40891" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48bf9793-96f8-52ed-b79d-6836af60287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394670} {:answer/id #uuid "ba63bba0-5be2-5771-96b3-596445281c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394671} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c4824c-4224-503f-a5d7-2b4a26578d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394672} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe05912-7854-59c8-bf0d-c8070c957702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394673} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4212ea-2b71-51c3-825c-dde5ba8c3781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48bf9793-96f8-52ed-b79d-6836af60287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394670} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a105006-9bf4-5c5a-8465-fcb6b86cf118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a38eea7-13c5-59fe-a820-3f0859b3b4fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-85275" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=12 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb3bac9-cce7-5665-8498-a91231b362d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351693} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a3d3a-a796-5667-954c-1ac273943f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351958} {:answer/id #uuid "37274342-d16b-5dbb-b2ca-57ddd587c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352080} {:answer/id #uuid "8bca07bf-2a3f-5acc-bea4-d63eb68cd5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352786} {:answer/id #uuid "b7338b51-8139-5d2f-8cea-d81f922627ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37274342-d16b-5dbb-b2ca-57ddd587c88b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352080} :answer-field/id #uuid "a221e58d-1676-5cff-be01-420fdcdb8b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c34a906-4ce9-5f9d-848e-084be407b879" :question/math-academy-id "q-85280" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59881df0-2ac6-532e-8fb9-91b698aa746f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352356} {:answer/id #uuid "6136a078-6ec6-579a-809a-d2077533b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352433} {:answer/id #uuid "76500562-20f2-5c7e-bdb3-4992adaf7d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352607} {:answer/id #uuid "7917c258-2cdc-5cb9-a21d-ebdf986ccad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{101}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352623} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352661} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e227d03-ecbf-552f-86b6-2f39084c0aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22b88813-fc85-5da7-b050-31934e04cdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85284" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c4d3c3-0803-5e7d-9345-96fb67a5c902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352058} {:answer/id #uuid "80911abe-21e9-5140-8653-0d040661f186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352689} {:answer/id #uuid "bbef92f5-bc23-5fdc-a91b-960a4232941f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353164} {:answer/id #uuid "e1942471-0602-51ba-8547-44bd01d58aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353523} {:answer/id #uuid "ec065dfd-7d91-51b7-91a4-a6cd26c12915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1942471-0602-51ba-8547-44bd01d58aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d7592d-5151-5ba8-a5b7-bd3172011f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a3f449-8731-5956-af27-4aa8fe25fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-85892" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc9412d-db1d-5f0b-bf46-1547fc9bc9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382561} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab9fcd-0d01-5a7c-a562-b332d1fa753b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} {:answer/id #uuid "2756ce9d-d692-57b4-88bb-5f748720577f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382564} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b1550-1387-5406-a483-f580bc72d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382563} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cb7faf-1c5d-5786-81e1-1833c5631b56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbf886ad-eced-5102-99df-0e48cd09970a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85905" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
